  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 340 AM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\n     TODAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY\n\nEarly this morning, water vapor satellite imagery and upper air \nanalysis show upper ridging across the south-central CONUS \nsandwiched between a pair of deep layer troughs across the \nnortheast CONUS and northern Rockies. The upper ridge will be \none one of the main drivers for warmer than average temperatures\nacross much of the forecast area through much of the week while\nthe trough across the northern Rockies will be one of the \nprimary factors in a stalled frontal boundary residing across \ncentral/northern Kansas through mid-week potentially leading to\nseveral rounds of showers and storms across these areas.\n\nFor today, strong southerly low-level flow will usher in a warmer \nairmass into the region. Additionally, a tight pressure gradient out \nahead of a slowly approaching cold front will lead to breezy/windy \nconditions across much of the forecast area. While mid-level winds \naren't crazy, deep mixing caused by strong diurnal heating will help \nto mix down wind gusts between 35-40 mph, especially this afternoon \nalong and west of I-135. Opted not to go with a wind advisory in \ncentral Kansas, but it's possible some locations could briefly see \nup to 45 mph wind gust for a couple of hours. In addition to the \nwindy conditions, highs across the entire forecast area will rise \ninto the mid to upper 90s. With unseasonably hot conditions, windy \nconditions, and dry vegetation (even with some recent rainfall), \nvery high grassland fire danger is likely for areas along and west \nof I-135 this afternoon. That being said, dewpoints in the low 60s \nshould mitigate fire weather concerns some.\n\nLater tonight into Tuesday morning, a sharp cold front will sag into \nnorthern Kansas. Ahead of a lob of vorticity aloft rounding the base \nof the deep layer trough over the northern CONUS, widespread showers \nand storms are expected to develop and overspread much of north-\ncentral and northeast Kansas. It's possible a couple storms sneak \ninto central Kansas, but much of the activity Monday night into \nearly Tuesday morning will remain along and north of I-70. There \ncould be a lull in precipitation from Tuesday morning into Tuesday \nafternoon. Then, strong diurnal heating along the frontal boundary \nalong with a weak and subtle upper trough moving east out of the \nHigh Plains should be enough to trigger additional showers and \nstorms across most locations except for far southeast Kansas. Based \non environmental instability (around 1500-2000 J/kg MLCAPE) and \nshear (effective shear around 20-25 knots), some strong to \nmarginally severe storms are possible with downbursts producing 50-\n60 mph wind gusts being the primary concern. PWATs between 1.7 and \n1.9 will support the potential for heavy rainfall as well. Some \ntraining of thunderstorms is possible, especially later in the \nevening hours across portions of central Kansas, which could lead to \nlocalized flooding. Storm chances have decreased for Wednesday, and \nthis makes since given the frontal boundary becoming more diffuse \nand the lack of synoptic support. In fact, much of the activity may \nbe limited to late Wednesday night when mid-level WAA ramps up on \nthe nose of the LLJ, but most showers and storms will be north of I-\n70 late Wednesday night into Thursday morning.\n\n     THURSDAY THROUGH SUNDAY\n\nThe forecast becomes a bit more uncertain towards the latter half of \nthe week, especially towards the weekend as the upper pattern \nbecomes a bit ill-defined. However, mid-range guidance this morning \nsuggests upper ridging across the southern CONUS briefly builds back \ninto the central plains on Thursday and Friday leading to afternoon \nhighs in the low to mid 90s, and lows in the low 70s, which is about \n10 degrees above average. Then, weak troughing lumbering across the \ncentral plains will be the catalyst for a cold front arriving late \nFriday/early Saturday. At the moment, the forecast calls for milder \ntemperatures for this weekend, but details are subject to change \nthis far out. Additionally, this cold front could also bring another \nshot at spotty chances for showers and storms.",
